<p class="sst-lead">Arc-length-weighted quadrature on \(N\) nodes reconstructs the shape's spectral data. We track two
errors against a heavily refined reference: the <strong>scale operator</strong> \(P\), and the
<strong>eigenfunctions</strong> \(v(s)=\bm{c}(s)\,A\,\mathrm{diag}(1/\sigma)\) &mdash; the
\(\bm{\alpha}\)-defined linear combinations, not just scale.</p>

<p>\[ \big\|P_N-P_{\mathrm{ref}}\big\|_F,\qquad \max_s\big\|v_N(s)-v_{\mathrm{ref}}(s)\big\| \;\longrightarrow\; 0. \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Two curves, side by side: an analytic <strong style="color:var(--color-accent-primary)">Cassini oval</strong>
  and a real segmented boundary, <strong style="color:#c9603f">apple-1</strong> (MPEG-7), as a periodic cubic
  spline. The analytic curve converges <strong>spectrally</strong> (a near-vertical plunge to the floor); the
  measured boundary, with only finite smoothness, converges <strong>algebraically</strong>.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">Both still beat the \(1/\sqrt{n}\) of Monte Carlo &mdash; what makes SST cheap enough for
  tens of thousands of shapes &mdash; but the honest picture is that real data does not enjoy the analytic rate.</p>
</div>
